Sprocket-Gong
Ingredients: K-POP + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Bells + 1980s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Bored film projectionists in an offshore tax haven scored phantom silent films on salvage-yard synthesizers and ship bells, accidentally engineering an aggressively synchronized idol music for an audience of machine ghosts.

Sprocket-Gong is the sound of hyper-synchronized Korean pop vocal choreography colliding with the abrasive clatter of 35mm cinema projectors, icy 1980s FM digital bells, and the rusted acoustic roar of industrial ship-warning gongs. It possesses the relentless, multi-hook vocal density of chart-topping Seoul dance music, yet its spine is forged from mechanical shutter flickers, analog tape flutter, and cavernous harbor reverberation.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 126–134 BPM. Relentless 4-on-the-floor kick drums interlaced with rapid 24-frames-per-second frame-rate tuplets (borrowed from projector shutter speeds) and machine-drilled 16th-note hi-hat grooves that shift abruptly into massive half-time militaristic dance breaks.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark Korean pyeonjong minor modes and Dorian tonalities layered with glistening Yamaha DX7 electric-tine chords; verses simmer in brooding suspended fourths before exploding into saccharine, hyper-melodic pentatonic major-key idol choruses.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Opto-Chime Gating: Bronze fog-bells and tubular carillons routed through photo-optical gates that open and close in sync with a 48Hz carbon-arc projector bulb, producing a violent, mechanical flutter.
  2. Nitrate-Emulsion Phasing (Impossible Technique): Running multi-part female vocal harmonies physically through an unspooled strip of 35mm film coated with liquid ferrofluid and seawater, dragging it across open optical soundheads at erratic hand-cranked velocities to generate an impossibly liquid, metallic unison shimmer that smears pitch microtonally without digital artifacts.
  3. Drydock Slapback: Gated snare reverb printed inside hollowed steel shipping containers using binaural boundary microphones, instantly snapping shut into bone-dry FM synth plucks.
- Dynamics: Extreme, whiplash theatricality. Tracks plummet from deafening, four-part choral climaxes backed by clattering iron bells into suffocating silences occupied only by the faint hum of a cooling turbine and a solitary, crystal-clear chime.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre emerged within the rotting maritime trade zone of Yeongdo, Busan, where automated freighter cranes rendered thousands of harbor hands obsolete. In the damp basements of decommissioned seaside movie palaces, night-shift projectionists tasked with archiving deteriorating film began creating live musical accompaniments for "blank reels"—discarded silent celluloid leader strips they pretended were lost avant-garde epics. Recruits from shuttered K-pop training academies joined them, trading trainee debt for the freedom of these subterranean screening rooms. The fashion of Sprocket-Gong reflects this collision: floor-length yellow maritime rain slickers over silver-lamé 1980s stage leotards, heavy iron-toed waders, safety goggles fitted with optical lens filters, and wristbands lined with steel picks for striking the tuned brass bells hung across the front of the projection booths. Audience members do not applaud; they signal approval by snapping handheld cinema clappers in rhythmic unisons.

Typical Instruments: Yamaha DX7 (preset 11 "E. PIANO 1" and custom FM bells), bronze maritime signal bells, Fairlight CMI Series IIx, Korean pyeonjong (tuned stone and bronze chimes), Roland Juno-60, Simmons SDS-V electronic drums, LinnDrum claps, optical soundhead microphones, pristine multi-tracked pop vocal ensembles.

